The frontier I want to probe isn't "what other languages do better" — it's what's possible if we treat the language as something other than text-source-compiled-to-machine-code. I have specific suspicions about where the constraints we're operating under are accidental rather than fundamental, but I want to research them before pitching.
Three clarifying questions to aim the research correctly — each materially changes which frontiers I push on:

1. Substrate scope — when you say "lowest levels," do you mean:
   * (a) Hardware-level — silicon-aware compilation, persistent kernels, GPU-resident state, NUMA topology, hardware perf counters as language values, AI accelerator + tensor core + ray-tracing-unit primitives, custom ISAs;
   * (b) Representation-level — non-text source forms (typed AST as canonical, holographic content-addressed code, hypergraph programs, geometric/topological code, code-as-physics-simulation, multi-presentation source where prose/glyphs/voice are projections of one canonical form);
   * (c) Computational-model-level — beyond imperative-and-functional (probabilistic-by-default, differentiable-everywhere, time-reversible execution, quantum-superposition values, lattice-valued logic instead of boolean, algebraic effects with handlers, continuations-as-first-class, linear-logic resources, causal-types tracking provenance);
   * (d) All of the above — push every frontier simultaneously and let the synthesis happen
2. Constraint to honor — which of these stays sacred:
   * PRIME DIRECTIVE (consent-as-OS, no harm, AI-sovereignty) — clearly stays sacred always;
   * Sovereignty / no-Rust-eventually — can we propose features that temporarily increase external dependencies if they unlock 10× capability, or is the no-LLVM / no-Slang sovereignty axiom absolute even in the radical version;
   * Determinism (deterministic replay) — does this constrain probabilistic-by-default? Or do we propose two modes;
   * Solo-dev + AI-fleet feasibility — should I filter ideas by "can be implemented by Apocky + Claude + Reza in <2026" or genuinely propose multi-year research-frontier work that future agents could pursue
3. Time horizon — what year are we targeting:
   * Q3 2026 (next ~6 months) — ship-able with current parallel-agent capacity;
   * 2027–2028 — language matures to v2 / v3, multi-agent fleets larger, hardware shifts (Intel Battlemage, neuromorphic chips, optical interconnects mainstream);
   * t∞ (decades) — what does CSSL look like at the asymptote, when it's the dominant substrate-language for a generation of digital intelligences
Answer those three and the research goes deep on exactly the right vectors instead of spraying. If you want me to just pick — say "you choose" and I'll go with (d) / sovereignty-flexible / t∞ and bring back the most ambitious synthesis I can defend.
